From: Donald Hoskins <grommish@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: dsa: turn off checksum and TSO offload on a nested conduit

A DSA tree may be conduited by a user port of another DSA tree, so that
frames for the inner tree carry two tags: one for the inner switch and
one for the outer. The Ubiquiti EdgeRouter 10X is built this way -- an
RTL8367C hangs off an MT7530 user port over RGMII, and tag_mtk has no
switch id field, so a single tree cannot address the second switch.

Nothing tells the Ethernet MAC that the frame it is about to transmit is
tagged twice. On MT7621 the checksum engine parses one MTK special tag,
finds the wrong offset for the L4 header on a doubly tagged frame, and
writes the checksum in the wrong place. Every locally generated TCP and
UDP packet leaves the board corrupt and peers drop it silently. The
symptom is that a host on the inner switch can ping the board and route
through it, but no TCP connection to it ever completes -- forwarded
traffic already carries a complete checksum and ICMP is checksummed in
software, so both keep working and hide the fault. The same applies to
segmentation: hardware TSO on a doubly tagged frame is equally wrong.

Fix it where the nesting becomes known. When a conduit is itself a DSA
user port, clear its checksum and segmentation features so the core
completes both in software in validate_xmit_skb() before the frame is
handed to the outer tree.

Doing it on the conduit rather than on the inner tree's user ports
matters. The user ports are bridge members, and a bridge advertises the
intersection of its members' features, so clearing them there would
strip offload from the bridge device and penalise the outer switch's
ports too. The conduit is not a bridge member, so the outer tree's user
ports keep hardware offload.

On the EdgeRouter 10X this is worth 410 -> 760 Mbit/s of board
terminated egress on the outer switch's ports, measured with iperf3,
while keeping the inner switch's ports correct.

Signed-off-by: Donald Hoskins <grommish@gmail.com>
--- a/include/net/dsa.h
+++ b/include/net/dsa.h
@@ -249,6 +249,12 @@
 		struct net_device *user;
 	};
 
+	/* Offload features stripped from the conduit because it is itself a
+	 * DSA user port, so frames leaving this tree are tagged twice. Kept
+	 * so they can be handed back on teardown. CPU ports only.
+	 */
+	netdev_features_t	conduit_stripped_features;
+
 	/* Copy of the tagging protocol operations, for quicker access
 	 * in the data path. Valid only for the CPU ports.
 	 */
--- a/net/dsa/conduit.c
+++ b/net/dsa/conduit.c
@@ -416,6 +416,37 @@
 			   "Unable to reset MTU to exclude DSA overheads\n");
 }
 
+/* A conduit that is itself a DSA user port means the frames it carries are
+ * tagged twice, once for this tree and once for the tree the conduit belongs
+ * to. An Ethernet MAC parsing a single tag will compute a checksum, or cut a
+ * TSO segment, at the wrong offset. Hand both back to the software path.
+ *
+ * This is deliberately applied to the conduit and not to this tree's user
+ * ports: those are bridge members, a bridge advertises the intersection of
+ * its members' features, and stripping them there would disable offload for
+ * the whole bridge -- including the ports of the tree above, which are singly
+ * tagged and perfectly able to use it.
+ */
+static void dsa_conduit_fixup_nested_features(struct net_device *dev,
+					      struct dsa_port *cpu_dp)
+{
+	netdev_features_t strip = NETIF_F_CSUM_MASK | NETIF_F_ALL_TSO;
+
+	if (!dsa_user_dev_check(dev))
+		return;
+
+	cpu_dp->conduit_stripped_features = dev->features & strip;
+	if (!cpu_dp->conduit_stripped_features)
+		return;
+
+	dev->features &= ~strip;
+	dev->hw_features &= ~strip;
+	dev->wanted_features &= ~strip;
+
+	netdev_info(dev,
+		    "nested DSA conduit, disabling checksum and TSO offload\n");
+}
+
 int dsa_conduit_setup(struct net_device *dev, struct dsa_port *cpu_dp)
 {
 	const struct dsa_device_ops *tag_ops = cpu_dp->tag_ops;
@@ -450,6 +481,8 @@
 	 */
 	wmb();
 
+	dsa_conduit_fixup_nested_features(dev, cpu_dp);
+
 	dev->dsa_ptr = cpu_dp;
 
 	dsa_conduit_set_promiscuity(dev, 1);
@@ -473,8 +506,18 @@
 
 void dsa_conduit_teardown(struct net_device *dev)
 {
+	struct dsa_port *cpu_dp = dev->dsa_ptr;
+
 	sysfs_remove_group(&dev->dev.kobj, &dsa_group);
 	dsa_conduit_ethtool_teardown(dev);
+
+	if (cpu_dp && cpu_dp->conduit_stripped_features) {
+		dev->features |= cpu_dp->conduit_stripped_features;
+		dev->hw_features |= cpu_dp->conduit_stripped_features;
+		dev->wanted_features |= cpu_dp->conduit_stripped_features;
+		cpu_dp->conduit_stripped_features = 0;
+	}
+
 	dsa_conduit_reset_mtu(dev);
 	dsa_conduit_set_promiscuity(dev, -1);
 
